          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Peak of the Revolt</strong><br>\nThe rebellion reached its peak around 72 BCE when Spartacus's forces defeated two Roman consular armies and overran Southern Italy. They ransacked the countryside, freeing slaves and gathering resources. At this time, Spartacus's army was estimated to have grown to over 120,000 men, women, and children.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cracks in the Revolt</strong><br>\nDespite their successes, the rebel slaves began to face challenges. Their diverse backgrounds and lack of common purpose beyond freedom led to divisions. Some wanted to continue fighting, while others wished to escape beyond the reach of Rome. Spartacus struggled to maintain unity and direct the rebellion's course.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The End of the Revolt and Legacy</strong><br>\nSpartacus's rebellion came to an end in 71 BCE when his army was finally defeated by a Roman force led by Marcus Licinius Crassus. Spartacus is believed to have died in the last battle, and his body was never found. His legacy endured as a symbol of the struggle for freedom and resistance against oppression, inspiring countless stories and legends.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
